=== Jungian archetypes ===

{{further|Character pairing in The Lord of the Rings}}

[[File:Patrick Grant's Jungian View of LOTR.svg|thumb|upright=1.5|Diagram of Patrick Grant's [[Carl Jung|Jungian]] view of [[Heroism in The Lord of the Rings|the hero]] Frodo with Galadriel as his anima, opposed by [[Shelob]]<ref name="Grant 1973"/>]]

Patrick Grant, a scholar of [[Renaissance]] literature, notes the multiple [[Character pairing in The Lord of the Rings|character pairings in ''The Lord of the Rings'']]. He interprets the interactions of the characters as fitting the oppositions and other pairwise relationships of [[Jungian archetypes]], recurring psychological symbols proposed by the psychotherapist [[Carl Jung]]. He states that the [[Heroism in The Lord of the Rings|hero]]'s quest can be interpreted as a personal journey of [[individuation]].  Galadriel functions as Frodo's [[Anima (Jung)|anima]], opposed by the evil giant female spider Shelob.<ref name="Grant 1973"/> Grant explains that the anima and animus represent "the feminine side of a man's unconscious, and the masculine side of a woman's, respectively."<ref name="Grant 1973"/> He adds that in the case of Tolkien's writing, the anima is more important, but also "ambivalent", both supportive and destructive. He gives as examples of the supportive and "nourishing" anima [[Dante Alighieri|Dante]]'s [[Beatrice Portinari|Beatrice]], the [[Muses]] of classical mythology who provided creative inspiration, and the [[Virgin Mary]]; on the destructive side, she can be symbolised, he writes, by the [[Siren (mythology)|siren of mythology]] who lures a man to disaster, or a "poisonous and malevolent" [[Witchcraft|witch]].<ref name="Grant 1973"/> Grant states that the anima and animus are "further from consciousness" than the [[Shadow (psychology)|shadow]] archetype.<ref name="Grant 1973"/> Both the anima/animus and the shadow are presented in conjunction with the hero archetype, signifying an "individuation process which is approaching wholeness".<ref name="Grant 1973"/> The set of archetypes creates an image of the self.<ref name="Grant 1973">{{cite journal |last=Grant |first=Patrick |title=Tolkien: Archetype and Word |journal=Cross Currents |date=1973 |issue=Winter 1973 |pages=365–380 |url=https://crosscurrents.org/tolkien.htm#_ednref9 |access-date=2020-08-19 |archive-date=2021-12-03 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203230011/http://www.crosscurrents.org/tolkien.htm#_ednref9 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Burns adds that the opposed characters of Galadriel and Shelob are indicated by elements such as the Phial of Galadriel, whose light contrasts with the darkness of the spider.<ref>{{cite book |last=Burns |first=Marjorie |author-link=Marjorie Burns |chapter=Double |editor-last=Drout |editor-first=Michael D.C. |editor-link=Michael D. C. Drout |title=[[The J. R. R. Tolkien Encyclopedia]] |publisher=[[Routledge]] |page=128}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Burns |first=Marjorie |author-link=Marjorie Burns |editor-last=Drout |editor-first=Michael D.C. |editor-link=Michael D. C. Drout |title=[[The J. R. R. Tolkien Encyclopedia]] |publisher=[[Routledge]] |pages=606 |chapter=Shelob}}</ref>

== Legacy in music ==

Tolkien wrote a poem "[[Namárië]]" that Galadriel sings in farewell to the departing Fellowship, and to Frodo in particular.  The song is in [[Quenya]], and "spoke of things little-known in Middle-earth," but Frodo is said to have remembered the words and translated them long afterward.  It is a lament in which Galadriel describes her separation from the [[Blessed Realm]] and the [[Valar]], her longing to return there, and at the end a wish or hope that even though she herself is forbidden (by the Ban) to return, that Frodo might somehow come in the end to the city of Valimar in [[Valinor]]. The poem was set to music by [[Donald Swann]], using the melody that Tolkien hummed to him. The sheet music and an audio recording are part of the song-cycle of ''[[The Road Goes Ever On]]''.<ref name="Swann's Foreword">{{harvnb<!--this is SECONDARY, as it's Swann's account-->|Tolkien|Swann|2002|pp=v–vi, Foreword by Donald Swann}}</ref> In a recording, Tolkien sings it in the style of a [[Gregorian chant]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Hargrove |first=Gene |title=Music in Middle-Earth |url=http://www.phil.unt.edu/~hargrove/music.html |date=January 1995 |access-date=8 July 2012 |publisher=[[University of North Texas]]}}</ref>

{{listen | title = Hijaz | filename = Arabic-scale hijaz.ogg | description = [[File:Hijaz scale.svg|frameless]] [[Howard Shore]] used the [[Arabic maqam]] [[Phrygian dominant scale|Hijaz scale]] to give the [[Lothlórien]]/Galadriel theme a sense of antiquity. | image=none}}

Galadriel's songs are omitted from [[Howard Shore]]'s [[Music of The Lord of the Rings film series|music for ''The Lord of the Rings'' film series]];<ref name="Jorgensen 2004">{{cite journal |last1=Jorgensen |first1=Estelle R. |title=Music, Myth, and Education: The Case of The Lord of the Rings Film Trilogy |journal=Reasons of the Heart: Myth, Meaning and Education, an International Conference, University of Edinburgh, September 9–12, 2004 |date=December 2004 |url=https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstream/handle/2022/27585/Music%20Myth%20and%20Education%20The%20Case%20of%20%20The%20Lord%20of%20the%20Rings%20Film%20Trilogy%20pre-print.pdf?sequence=1 |pages=6–7 |archive-date=2022-12-27 |access-date=2022-12-27 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221227152611/https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/bitstream/handle/2022/27585/Music%20Myth%20and%20Education%20The%20Case%20of%20%20The%20Lord%20of%20the%20Rings%20Film%20Trilogy%20pre-print.pdf?sequence=1 |url-status=dead }}</ref> instead, Shore created a Lothlórien/Galadriel theme using the  [[Arabic maqam]] [[Phrygian dominant scale|Hijaz]] scale to create a sense of antiquity.{{efn|name="Lothlórien"|[http://www.woodzie.org/lotr/themes/elves.mp3 Lothlórien/Galadriel theme] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070311214527/http://www.woodzie.org/lotr/themes/elves.mp3 |date=11 March 2007 }} (listening sample) }}<ref>{{cite book |last=Adams |first=Doug |author-link=Doug Adams (music journalist) |title=The Music of The Lord of the Rings Films |publisher=Carpentier |year=2010 |isbn=978-0-7390-7157-1 |pages=51–54}}</ref>
[[Fran Walsh]], Shore, and [[Annie Lennox]] co-wrote the Oscar-winning song [[Into the West (song)|"Into the West"]] for the closing credits of ''[[The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King]]''. Originally sung by Lennox, the song was conceived as Galadriel's bittersweet lament for those who have sailed across the [[Belegaer|Sundering Seas]]. The lyrics include phrases from the final chapter of the original novel. The song has since been covered by [[Yulia Townsend]] and [[Will Martin]].<ref>{{cite book |last=Roberts |first=David |year=2006 |title=British Hit Singles & Albums |edition=19th |publisher=[[Guinness World Records]] |isbn=978-1904994107 |page=137}}</ref>

On their album ''[[Once Again (Barclay James Harvest album)|Once Again]]'', the band [[Barclay James Harvest]] featured a song called "Galadriel". It gained notability because guitarist [[John Lees (musician)|John Lees]] played [[John Lennon]]'s [[Epiphone Casino]] guitar on this track, an event later recounted in a song on the band's 1990 album ''Welcome To The Show'' titled "John Lennon's Guitar".<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.bjharvest.co.uk/once.htm#galadriel |title=Galadriel |publisher=[[Barclay James Harvest]] |access-date=12 May 2020}}</ref>
[[Hank Marvin]] and John Farrar wrote a song "Galadriel", recorded by [[Cliff Richard]]; the four five-line stanzas include the couplet "Galadriel, spirit of starlight / Eagle and dove gave birth to thee".<ref>{{cite book |author=Library of Congress Copyright Office |title=Catalog of Copyright Entries: Third series |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=H10hAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA1618 |year=1977 |page=1618}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Gramophone |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=axRMAAAAYAAJ |year=1983 |publisher=C. Mackenzie |volume=61 |page=541}}</ref> An Australian band named [[Galadriel (Australian band)|Galadriel]] released a self-titled album in 1971 which "became a highly sought-after collectors' item among European progressive rock circles".<ref name="McFarlane">{{cite book |last=McFarlane |first=Ian |author-link=Ian McFarlane |title=[[Encyclopedia of Australian Rock and Pop]] |chapter=Broderick Smith |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040803173727/http://www.whammo.com.au/encyclopedia.asp?articleid=5 |chapter-url=http://www.whammo.com.au/encyclopedia.asp?articleid=5 |year=1999 |publisher=[[Allen & Unwin]] |archive-date=3 August 2004 |isbn=1-86508-072-1 |access-date=23 August 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>

